@node	4.3, 4.4, 4.2, Chapter 4
@section Nondeterministic Computing

In this section, we extend the evaluator to support a programming
paradigm called @newterm{nondeterministic computing} by building into the
evaluator a facility to support automatic search.  This is a much more profound
change to the language than the introduction of lazy evaluation in
@ref{4.2}.  In this edition the evaluator of the section is the module
@code{ch04::sec_4_3}, and the search machinery is an explicit
backtracking engine over typed data: the named experiment
@code{search-depth-first/1} explores @code{Choose}, @code{Fail}, and
@code{Success} values depth-first in the vector's written order, records every
assignment on an explicit trail, and rolls back only trailed assignments when
it backtracks.  It is deterministic for a fixed program, and its answers are
checked against the independent reference model @code{reference_model}.  This
is a property of the experiment only: @code{?}, @code{Result}, panics, and
ordinary @code{return} never trigger search backtracking in the guest
language.

Nondeterministic computing, like stream processing, is useful for ``generate
and test'' applications.  Consider the task of starting with two lists of
positive integers and finding a pair of integers---one from the first list and
one from the second list---whose sum is prime.  We saw how to handle this with
finite sequence operations in @ref{2.2.3} and with infinite streams in
@ref{3.5.3}.  Our approach was to generate the sequence of all possible pairs
and filter these for those whose sum is prime.  In the nondeterministic
approach, we say that we will pick an element from the first list and an
element from the second list, and require that their sum be prime.  This
approach can be more natural, because it says nothing about the order in
which pairs are tried:

@example
element = Choose(first_list),
second = Choose(second_list),
Guard(SumEq([element, second], @r{…}), Success([element, second]))
@end example

@noindent
The engine carries the choice, the constraint, and the answer as data.  If the
first pair fails the guard, the trail restores the bindings and the next pair
is tried; the successful pair is answered in the order the search produced it.
The programmer states the constraints, and the engine searches
``automagically.''@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 250}Automagically: ``Automatically, but
in a way which, for some reason (typically because it is too complicated, or
too ugly, or perhaps even too trivial), the speaker doesn't feel like
explaining.''  (@ref{Steele et al. 1983}, @ref{Raymond 1993})}

The multiple-dwelling puzzle of @ref{4.3.2} is a larger example of the same
shape: one choice point per resident, one guard per restriction, and one
answer tuple for the assignment that survives.  The restrictions can be tried
in any order; @ref{Exercise 4.39} asks what that freedom costs.

