* 4.3.1::            Search and Choice
* 4.3.2::            Examples of Nondeterministic Programs
* 4.3.3::            Implementing the Search Evaluator
@end menu

@node	4.3.1, 4.3.2, 4.3, 4.3
@subsection Search and Choice

To extend the object language to support nondeterminism, we introduce a
choice form.@footnote{The idea of nondeterministic choice in programming was
first described in 1961 by John McCarthy (see @ref{McCarthy 1963}).}  In this
edition the choice form is not new Rust syntax: the experiment's programs are
typed data, and a choice is the constructor

@example rust
Search::Choose(Vec<Search>)
@end example

@noindent
which enumerates its alternatives in the order they are written.  An
expression with many values is a tree of choices; a dead end is

@example rust
Search::Fail
@end example

@noindent
and a produced answer is @code{Search::Success}, whose parts are computed from
the bindings the search has made:

@example rust
Search::Success(vec![AnswerTerm::Var(String::from("i"))])
@end example

@noindent
To run the same generate-and-test shape in the engine, we choose one integer
from each list, and a guard decides which choices survive.  The guard
vocabulary has no primality test, so the experiment stands in equality to one
prime target, 17, for the general prime-sum check:

@example rust
let program = Search::Choose(vec![
    Search::ChooseRange @{
        var: String::from("x"),
        lo: 1,
        hi: 10,
        body: Box::new(Search::ChooseRange @{
            var: String::from("y"),
            lo: 1,
            hi: 10,
            body: Box::new(Search::Guard(
                Predicate::SumEq(
                    vec![
                        Term::Variable(String::from("x")),
                        Term::Variable(String::from("y")),
                    ],
                    17,
                ),
                Box::new(Search::Success(vec![
                    AnswerTerm::Var(String::from("x")),
                    AnswerTerm::Var(String::from("y")),
                ])),
            )),
        @}),
    @},
]);
let mut engine = SearchEngine::new();
let outcome = engine.run(&program);
@end example

@noindent
Here @code{x} ranges over the inclusive bounds of @code{ChooseRange}, the
guard admits exactly the pairs that sum to 17, and the answers are the pairs
in depth-first written order.

@subsubheading The mechanics of choice

A choice point is a request to the engine to try one alternative, and, if
that alternative fails, to restore the state to the moment of the choice and
try the next one.  The engine implements this with an explicit @newterm{trail}:
every assignment records the name and the old value it displaced, and
backtracking pops trail entries until the bindings are as they were at the
choice.  Two kinds of assignment are distinguished from the start:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Search::Set(name, value, body)} is an ordinary assignment: when the
search crosses it going backwards, the old value is restored.

@item
@code{Search::Persist(name, value, body)} is a permanent assignment: the
trail never restores it (see @ref{Exercise 4.51}).

@end itemize

@noindent
Constraints are data as well.  @code{Search::Guard(predicate, body)} runs the
body only when the predicate holds over the current bindings, and the
predicate vocabulary of the query module covers the tests the puzzles need:
@code{Eq}, @code{Ne}, @code{Lt}, @code{Le}, @code{Gt}, @code{Ge},
@code{TextLt}, @code{SumEq}, @code{Bound}, the disjunction @code{Or}, the
difference test @code{DiffEq(a, b, c, d)} over four bound names for @math{{a - b = c - d}}, and
@code{Pythagorean(a, b, c)} for @math{{a^2 + b^2 = c^2}}.  An ordered effect
that the log must record is @code{Search::Emit(tag, body)}.

@subsubheading Trying again

The driver of the book's evaluator answers one value and then accepts a
request for the next one.  In this edition the same behavior is observable in
one run: @code{SearchEngine::run} answers every success in search order, and
@code{SearchEngine::run_prefix(program, n)} answers at most @math{n} of
them---the observable prefix discipline that makes unbounded generators
finite to inspect.  Each call explores the program afresh, so the book's
``try again'' is observed as successive prefixes of one deterministic
enumeration rather than as a saved continuation; the alternatives are held as
data instead of as a second continuation.

@quotation
@strong{In this language: automatic search.}  The book's search is woven into
the evaluator through success and failure continuations; this edition keeps
the variation in the named experiment @code{search-depth-first/1}, where the
alternatives are data.  A @code{Choose} node holds its alternatives in
written order, the engine marks the trail before each one and rolls the trail
back to the mark afterwards, and only trailed assignments are undone---which
is exactly the book's undo trail.  @code{Persist} is the book's
@code{permanent-set!}; @code{Guard} is @code{require}; @code{run_prefix} is
the driver's ``try again'' made into a finite observation.  The reference
model @code{reference_model} is a separate enumerator with its own trail and
answer bookkeeping, so answer order and rollback discipline are checked
against something other than the engine's own state.  The core evaluator is
unchanged: ordinary Rust never backtracks, and the experiment is selected
explicitly.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.35}Exercise 4.35:} Write a procedure
@code{an-integer-between} that returns an integer between two given bounds.
In this edition the engine supplies the generator as
@code{Search::ChooseRange}, which binds its variable over an inclusive range.
Use it to implement a search that finds Pythagorean triples, i.e.,
triples of integers @math{{(i, j, k)}} between the given bounds such that
@math{{i \le j}} and @math{{i^2 + j^2 = k^2}}, as follows:

@example
i = ChooseRange(low, high),
j = ChooseRange(i, high),
k = ChooseRange(j, high),
Guard(Pythagorean(i, j, k), Success([i, j, k]))
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.36}Exercise 4.36:} @ref{Exercise 3.69} discussed how
to generate the stream of @emph{all} Pythagorean triples, with no upper bound
on the size of the integers to be searched.  Explain why simply replacing
@code{an-integer-between} by @code{an-integer-starting-from}---the unbounded
generator @code{Search::ChooseFrom}---in the procedure
of @ref{Exercise 4.35} is not an adequate way to generate arbit@-rary Py@-tha@-go@-rean
triples.  Write a procedure that actually will accomplish this.  (That is,
write a procedure for which repeatedly asking for the next answer---observing
