      Guard(Le(k, high), Success([i, j, k])))
@end example
@end quotation

@node	4.3.2, 4.3.3, 4.3.1, 4.3
@subsection Examples of Nondeterministic Programs

Section @ref{4.3.3} describes the implementation of the search evaluator.
First, however, we give some examples of how it can be used.  The advantage of
nondeterministic programming is that we can suppress the details of how search
is carried out, thereby expressing our programs at a higher level.

@subsubheading Logic puzzles

The multiple-dwelling puzzle: Baker, Cooper, Fletcher, Miller, and Smith live
on different floors of a five-floor apartment house.  Baker does not live on
the top floor.  Cooper does not live on the bottom floor.  Fletcher does not
live on either the top or the bottom floor.  Miller lives on a higher floor
than Cooper.  Smith does not live on a floor adjacent to Fletcher.  Fletcher
does not live on a floor adjacent to Cooper.  The search program is one
choice per resident and one guard per restriction:

@example rust
fn multiple_dwelling() -> Search @{
    let floors = |name: &str| Search::ChooseRange @{
        var: String::from(name),
        lo: 1,
        hi: 5,
        body: Box::new(@r{…}),
    @};
    @r{…}
    // One Guard per restriction, then the answer.  A DiffEq test
    // compares two differences over bound names only, so the constant
    // differences are bound first and each adjacency exclusion is
    // an Or over the six non-adjacent differences (two, three, or
    // four floors either way):
    // Set(c0, 0, Set(c2, 2, Set(c3, 3, Set(c4, 4,
    // Guard(Ne(baker, 5),
    // Guard(Ne(cooper, 1),
    // Guard(Ne(fletcher, 1),
    // Guard(Ne(fletcher, 5),
    // Guard(Lt(cooper, miller),
    // Guard(Or([DiffEq(smith, fletcher, c2, c0),
    //           DiffEq(smith, fletcher, c3, c0),
    //           DiffEq(smith, fletcher, c4, c0),
    //           DiffEq(smith, fletcher, c0, c2),
    //           DiffEq(smith, fletcher, c0, c3),
    //           DiffEq(smith, fletcher, c0, c4)]),
    // Guard(Or(@r{…} the same six differences for fletcher, cooper),
    // Success([baker, cooper, fletcher, miller, smith]))))))))))))
    @r{…}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The restrictions are @code{Ne} tests for the floor exclusions, @code{Lt} for
Miller above Cooper, and one @code{Or} of @code{DiffEq} tests for each
adjacency exclusion: @code{DiffEq(a, b, c, d)} holds exactly when the two
differences @math{a - b} and @math{c - d} are equal over four bound names, so
each exclusion admits only the non-adjacent differences.  The evaluator
enumerates assignments of
people to floors and imposes the restrictions one at a time; the one
assignment that survives is the book's answer.  Although this simple program
works, it is very slow.  @ref{Exercise 4.39} and @ref{Exercise 4.40} discuss
some possible improvements.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.38}Exercise 4.38:} Modify the multiple-dwelling
program to omit the requirement that Smith and Fletcher do not live on
adjacent floors.  How many solutions are there to this modified puzzle?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.39}Exercise 4.39:} Does the order of the
restrictions in the multiple-dwelling program affect the answer? Does it
affect the time to find an answer?  If you think it matters, demonstrate a
faster program obtained from the given one by reordering the restrictions.  If
you think it does not matter, argue your case.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.40}Exercise 4.40:} In the multiple dwelling problem,
how many sets of assignments are there of people to floors, both before and
after the requirement that floor assignments be distinct?  It is very
inefficient to generate all possible assignments of people to floors and then
leave it to backtracking to eliminate them.  For example, most of the
restrictions depend on only one or two of the person-floor variables, and can
thus be imposed before floors have been selected for all the people.  Write
and demonstrate a much more efficient nondeterministic program that solves
this problem based upon generating only those possibilities that are not
