@subsubheading Parsing natural language

Programs designed to accept natural language as input usually start by
attempting to @newterm{parse} the input, that is, to match the input against
some grammatical structure.  For example, we might try to recognize simple
sentences consisting of an article followed by a noun followed by a verb, such
as ``The cat eats.''  To accomplish such an analysis, we must be able to
identify the parts of speech of individual words.  We could start with typed
data that classify various words:@footnote{Here we use the convention that the
tag of each classification designates the part of speech for the rest of the
words in it.}

@example rust
let nouns: Vec<Word> = vec![
    word(Part::Noun, "student"),
    word(Part::Noun, "professor"),
    word(Part::Noun, "cat"),
    word(Part::Noun, "class"),
];
let verbs: Vec<Word> = vec![/* studies lectures eats sleeps */];
let articles: Vec<Word> = vec![/* the a */];
@end example

@noindent
We also need a @newterm{grammar}, that is, a set of rules describing how
grammatical elements are composed from simpler elements.  A very simple grammar
might stipulate that a sentence always consists of two pieces---a noun phrase
followed by a verb---and that a noun phrase consists of an article followed by
a noun.  With this grammar, the sentence ``The cat eats'' is parsed as typed
tree data:

@example
Sentence(
  NounPhrase(Article(the), Noun(cat)),
  Verb(eats))
@end example

@noindent
We can generate such a parse with a simple program that has separate procedures
for each of the grammatical rules.  To parse a sentence, we identify its two
constituent pieces and construct the two-element tree tagged @code{Sentence}.
A noun phrase, similarly, is parsed by finding an article followed by a
noun.  At the lowest level, parsing boils down to repeatedly checking that the
next unparsed word is a member of the list of words for the required part of
speech.  To implement this, we maintain the input that has not yet been parsed
in a trailed binding @code{unparsed}---an index into the word vector.  Each
time we check a word, we require that @code{unparsed} be in range and that the
word there belong to the designated part of speech.  If so, we advance
@code{unparsed} and return the word together with its part of speech:
@footnote{Notice that @code{parse_word} assigns to the unparsed input
index.  For this to work, our search must undo the effects of the assignment
when it backtracks---which is exactly the trailed @code{Search::Set}.}

@example rust
// parse_word(part): bind "word" to the next input index, guard that
// the word there has the required part of speech, then Set("unparsed",
// index + 1, Success(...)) on the accepted path.
let parse_word = |part: Part| Search::ChooseRange @{
    var: String::from("word"),
    lo: 0,
    hi: last_index,
    body: Box::new(Search::Guard(
        word_has_part("word", part),
        Box::new(Search::Set(
            String::from("unparsed"),
            next_index,
            Box::new(Search::Success(vec![word_of("word")])),
        )),
    )),
@};
@end example

@noindent
To start the parsing, all we need to do is set @code{unparsed} to the
beginning of the input, try to parse a sentence, and check that nothing is
left over:

@example rust
// parse(input): Set("unparsed", 0, body), parse a sentence, then
// Guard(Bound(unparsed_is_at_the_end), the sentence's tree).
@end example

@noindent
We can now try the parser and verify that it works for our simple test
sentence:

@example
parse(the cat eats)
// => Sentence(NounPhrase(Article(the), Noun(cat)), Verb(eats))
@end example

@noindent
The search experiment is useful here because it is convenient to express the
parsing constraints with the aid of guards.  Automatic search and
backtracking really pay off, however, when we consider more complex grammars
where there are choices for how the units can be decomposed.

Let's add to our grammar a list of prepositions (``for'', ``to'', ``in'',
``by'', ``with'') and define a prepositional phrase (e.g., ``for the cat'') to
be a preposition followed by a noun phrase.  Now we can define a sentence to
be a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase, where a verb phrase can be either
a verb or a verb phrase extended by a prepositional phrase:@footnote{Observe
that this definition is recursive---a verb may be followed by any number of
prepositional phrases.}

@example
VerbPhrase(verb)
VerbPhrase(verb_phrase, PrepPhrase(prep, noun_phrase))
@end example

@noindent
While we're at it, we can also elaborate the definition of noun phrases to
permit such things as ``a cat in the class.''  What we used to call a noun
phrase, we'll now call a simple noun phrase, and a noun phrase will now be
either a simple noun phrase or a noun phrase extended by a prepositional
phrase.  The choice points are the alternative decompositions, and each
expansion is a @code{Choose} of its two shapes.

@noindent
Our new grammar lets us parse more complex sentences.  For example

@example
parse(the student with the cat sleeps in the class)
// => Sentence(
//      NounPhrase(
//        SimpleNounPhrase(Article(the), Noun(student)),
//        PrepPhrase(Prep(with),
//                   SimpleNounPhrase(Article(the), Noun(cat)))),
//      VerbPhrase(
//        Verb(sleeps),
//        PrepPhrase(Prep(in),
//                   SimpleNounPhrase(Article(the), Noun(class)))))
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.45}Exercise 4.45:} With the grammar given above,
the following sentence can be parsed in five different ways: ``The professor
lectures to the student in the class with the cat.''  Write a search program
that returns all five parses as tree data, and explain why the grammar is
ambiguous for this sentence.
@end quotation

