@node	4.3.3, 4.4, 4.2, 4.3
@subsection Implementing the Search Evaluator

The evaluation of an ordinary program may return a value, may never
terminate, or may signal a trap.  In the search experiment the exploration of
a program may in addition result in the discovery of a dead end, in which case
the search must backtrack to a previous choice point.  This section describes
the engine that implements this behavior over the typed data of
@ref{4.3.1}.

@subsubheading Alternatives and the trail

The engine keeps one explicit trail and one ordered effect log:

@example rust
pub struct SearchEngine @{
    trail: Vec<(String, Option<i64>)>,
    bindings: HashMap<String, i64>,
    answers: Vec<Vec<AnswerValue>>,
    effects: Vec<String>,
    limit: usize,
@}
@end example

@noindent
Every assignment records the name and the value it displaced:

@example rust
fn bind(&mut self, name: &str, value: i64) @{
    let previous = self.bindings.insert(name.to_owned(), value);
    self.trail.push((name.to_owned(), previous));
@}
@end example

@noindent
The trail rolls back in one place.  When the search unwinds to a choice (a
failed alternative, or the end of an alternative's reach), the engine restores
every entry above the choice's mark:

@example rust
fn rollback(&mut self, mark: usize) @{
    while self.trail.len() > mark @{
        let Some((name, previous)) = self.trail.pop() else @{
            break;
        @};
        match previous @{
            Some(value) => @{
                self.bindings.insert(name, value);
            @}
            None => @{
                self.bindings.remove(&name);
            @}
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
This is the book's undo trail, and it is why @code{parse_word}'s assignment of
@ref{4.3.2} works: the failed parse restores the input index before the next
alternative runs.  The permanent assignment of @ref{Exercise 4.51} records
nothing on the trail:

@example rust
Search::Persist(name, value, body) => @{
    self.bindings.insert(name.clone(), *value);
    self.explore(body);
@},
@end example

@subsubheading Evaluating choice expressions

The exploration of a choice is the heart of the search.  The alternatives are
tried in written order; the mark is taken before each one and the trail is
rolled back to the mark after it, so no alternative sees the mutations of the
alternative before it:

@example rust
Search::Choose(alternatives) => @{
    for alternative in alternatives @{
        if self.answers.len() >= self.limit @{
            return;
        @}
        let mark = self.trail.len();
        self.explore(alternative);
        self.rollback(mark);
    @}
@},
@end example

@noindent
The generators are the same loop with the binding made for each generated
value; @code{ChooseRange} is @code{an-integer-between} and stops at its
inclusive bound, @code{ChooseFrom} is
@code{an-integer-starting-from} and runs until the answer limit stops it:

@example rust
Search::ChooseRange @{ var, lo, hi, body @} => @{
    let mut value = *lo;
    while value <= *hi @{
        if self.answers.len() >= self.limit @{
            return;
        @}
        self.bind(var, value);
        self.explore(body);
        self.rollback_binding(var);
        value = value.saturating_add(1);
    @}
@},
@end example

@noindent
A guard runs its body only when its predicate holds over the current bindings,
and an effect is logged before its body runs.  A success computes its answer
from the current bindings and records it:

@example rust
Search::Success(parts) => @{
    if let Some(answer) = self.resolve_answer(parts) @{
        self.answers.push(answer);
    @}
@},
Search::Fail => @{ @},
@end example

@noindent
Failure is therefore not an exception: it is simply a branch that produces no
answers, and the caller's rollback discipline restores the state before the
next alternative.  This is why the experiment is deterministic for a fixed
program and why ordinary Rust control flow never triggers backtracking.

@subsubheading Trying again and answer limits

The book's driver loop answers one value and then accepts a request for the
next one.  Here the same computation is answered as an ordered collection:
@code{run} explores until the program is exhausted, and @code{run_prefix}
stops after the requested number of answers, which is what makes an unbounded
generator like @code{ChooseFrom} observable as a finite prefix.  The answers
are computed from the bindings at the moment of the success, so an answer can
carry values the search computed before it (see the @code{Sum},
@code{Product}, and @code{Atom} parts of @code{AnswerTerm}).

@subsubheading The reference model

The engine is not its own oracle.  @code{reference_model} is an independent
finite enumerator with its own trail and answer bookkeeping; the conformance
checks compare the engine's answer order, rollback discipline, and effect log
against it:

@example rust
pub fn reference_model(program: &Search) -> SearchOutcome @{
    @r{…} // a separate depth-first enumerator
@}
@end example

